[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189671] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189672] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189673] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189674] [:db/retract 17592186189670 :answer-field/choices 17592186189675] {:db/id 17592186192696 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" "question-39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192964 :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $10$ and $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186189670 :answer-field/correct "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer-field/choices ["answer-293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" "answer-7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" "answer-ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" "answer-c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/id #uuid "293116cd-0210-5cb9-b17d-b36dfb030db8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/id #uuid "7d7115fa-f1a5-50b1-8fb7-1bae6211fe0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/id #uuid "ad077701-5e2a-550b-be10-c54987ab6c1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/id #uuid "c3d6cc6f-6996-5a19-a1b9-8d08475ff929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/id #uuid "0f6ce6b3-e086-5afc-bc5f-380578a38364" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/id #uuid "43345541-f657-59ff-989d-45d4cfc772b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138506" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1$ and $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "44831ce7-23e4-5530-a096-5c88232103b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" "answer-ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" "answer-2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" "answer-756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/id #uuid "af6affa2-ae7d-5319-81fa-de2ad83b73eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/id #uuid "ed501507-7e1b-5c82-a8f9-b2410c792d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/id #uuid "2c97f85d-47a1-51b6-a9b9-778680ce8fdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/id #uuid "18bbaa38-d421-5746-a3c1-8145fc019ca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/id #uuid "756ee275-5880-5a22-a4a6-9e1067a5ba8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/id #uuid "39ac48e2-8478-529d-91f9-60e771cc4eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-138510" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the second principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $13$ and $3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are ${\\lambda }_{1}=13$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 12\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa5eccd0-37fc-5413-a3f2-96535a173581" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" "answer-2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" "answer-26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" "answer-a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" "answer-20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/id #uuid "b2d2312f-9076-523c-ae85-c86bf3c40aa2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/id #uuid "2252d4c1-be79-5250-8ae4-9fec316b035e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/id #uuid "26c057ba-2bda-5aa2-9455-8957654b4964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/id #uuid "a7da3ea0-6b29-5fcd-8803-c68581c9ecb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/id #uuid "20807156-9f5d-5d9d-96f8-62147aa1f8b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192681 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" "question-acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/id #uuid "a946e21d-5a37-5585-a234-01e6acc34eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138515" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the first principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $1,$ $2,$ and $9.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=9,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/id #uuid "105a3af8-27bf-5111-9830-51162d55cab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer-field/choices ["answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" "answer-9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" "answer-e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" "answer-097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" "answer-cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/id #uuid "482967d1-9890-55e3-9174-ae742791f383" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/id #uuid "9756f17c-547d-5717-b4bf-797326722482" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/id #uuid "e1618446-0931-52ac-b916-219dfe148992" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/id #uuid "097cd6bb-a98e-56ad-9b47-98d16bfbaa02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/id #uuid "cbb420df-0b0d-5edc-867c-19a182ed465c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/id #uuid "acbab80b-b003-5343-8102-3dfe5d2664d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-138532" :question/problem "The covariance matrix $C$ of some particular observations is shown below. \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the third principal component of the data, given that the eigenvalues of $C$ are $8,$ $5,$ and $2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>⋯>{\\lambda }_{n}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${u}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nand so on.\n\nIn our case, the eigenvalues of the covariance matrix $C,$ in descending order, are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(C-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=3,$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b6d3086-6051-52d2-aff8-a787552ec9a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" "answer-c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" "answer-cff31548-be96-50f5-b36c-0864299f187b" "answer-d77ccd62-a3e0-5ccb-86f5-b0e37d719ee6" "answer-2a0b92ff-1a4f-5a5c-880f-dccb4e0edd45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/id #uuid "f878ea67-ef32-52af-ab6f-9dbc92982da8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/id #uuid "c7f8f5f8-4566-550b-af9b-f5286609b0bd" :answer/ty